<div class="q-page">
  <QuorumWeb.Shell.header current_user={@conn.assigns[:current_user]} />

  <main class="q-prose">
    <p class="q-draft">
      This is a working draft.
    </p>

    <h1>Accessibility</h1>
    <p class="q-prose-lead">
      Quorum aims at WCAG 2.2 AA. An inaccessible tool in a full room shuts someone out of the
      class itself, so this is built in as each screen is built rather than added afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>What's in place</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every action is reachable from the keyboard, and the focus ring is always visible.</li>
      <li>
        The presenter's console carries its own keyboard map on screen: <strong>J</strong>
        and <strong>K</strong>
        move through the queue, <strong>Enter</strong>
        spotlights, <strong>A</strong>
        marks answered, <strong>H</strong>
        hides. The projection uses <strong>L</strong>, <strong>D</strong>, and <strong>Q</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Colour is never the only signal. A question you voted on says "Voted" in words, an answered
        one says "Voting closed", and the spotlighted row says "On the projection now".
      </li>
      <li>
        Every control is at least 44 by 44 pixels, including text links that act as buttons.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every colour pairing in the palette has been measured, and all of them clear 4.5:1,
        including against the lightest end of the projection's gradient.
      </li>
      <li>
        Custom controls carry the semantics of what they imitate, so the vote control is a button with a label that reads its count and its state.
      </li>
      <li>
        The projection's background drift stops for anyone who has asked for reduced motion.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every page works from a 375 pixel phone up, no page scrolls sideways, and browser zoom
        scales the whole layout.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Known gaps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No independent audit has been done. The claims above are from our own testing.</li>
      <li>
        Screen reader testing has covered the structure and labels, not yet a full pass with each major reader.
      </li>
      <li>
        Single sign-on isn't available yet, so a presenter whose institution requires it can't use their usual route in.
      </li>
      <li>
        There's no captioning or transcript feature, because Quorum handles text questions rather than audio.
      </li>
      <li>
        Type is set in pixels, so it follows browser zoom but not a raised default font size in
        the browser's own settings. Moving to relative units is on the list.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Found a barrier?</h2>
    <p>
      Tell us and we'll fix it. Say which screen, what you were trying to do, and what got in the
      way. Reports about a barrier are treated as bugs, not feature requests.
    </p>

    <p class="q-prose-cta">
      <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="q-button">Report an accessibility problem</.link>
    </p>
  </main>

  <QuorumWeb.Shell.footer />
</div>
